        "checklist": {
          "capacity": {
            "status": "ok",
            "note": "Capacity is local, not national. Rock is abundant but freight is roughly a third of delivered cost, so the economic haul radius is near 25 miles and each quarry serves its own market. National share is close to meaningless; what matters is being #1 or #2 within the radius, and how many years of permitted reserves sit inside it."
          },
          "pricing": {
            "status": "ok",
            "note": "Pricing has held through a volume downturn, which is the whole thesis. Crushed-stone PPI is +6.0% year over year and VMC's freight-adjusted price is +4.3% to $21.98/ton, while single-family starts are -15.7%. Freight-adjusted is the only comparable measure: unadjusted ASP tracks diesel, not pricing power."
          },
          "regulatory": {
            "status": "permissioning",
            "note": "Zoning and permitting are simultaneously the moat and the tail risk. New quarries near a growing metro are close to un-permittable, which protects incumbents and deepens the moat over time; the same process can contest a renewal on one critical site, and a company-average reserve life hides that. Aggregates M&A also draws antitrust divestiture conditions."
          }
        }
